(413745, 35041, 1005, '<td valign="top">Solid photo, no glare spots on the bucket (very good job!) and still the lighting is good.  A bit overly centered again....I like the face on the handle latch...might have been a good macro subject.  <br/> <br/>To answer your question in the Photographer''s Comments...same way you fool birds, build a blind.  <br/>I used a very clumsy blind for this shot:<br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=9109" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=9109</a> <br/>See the camera?  (The shot was supposed to make you laugh!!)</td>', 'Solid photo, no glare spots on the bucket (very good job!) and still the lighting is good.  A bit overly centered again....I like the face on the handle latch...might have been a good macro subject.   To answer your question in the Photographer''s Comments...same way you fool birds, build a blind.  I used a very clumsy blind for this shot://www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=9109 See the camera?  (The shot was supposed to make you laugh!!)', '2003-09-09 19:42:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(413791, 35218, 6202, '<td valign="top">*Critique CLub*<br/> <br/>Your idea here is very good, it would make for some good photojournalism but it is to soft.  You need to sharpen it up a bit.  Tools was the challenge subject and the tool in this case appears to be a baseball bat, and it should be the main subject not a blurry little area of the photo.<br/> <br/>Looking at the photo in a fast glance many voters would have given you a low score not seeing the baseball bat.  Might I strongly suggest you don''t use "Untitled" as a subject.  I feel a photo without a title is missing part of it''s personality as well as part of the photographers personality.  As I look at it I would easily title it something like "Meet my brother the Louisville Slugger" Simply knowing most people know that a Louisville Slugger is a bat, it would make the average voter stop and realize there was a bat in the photo and that is what you wanted the voter to recognize as the tool.<br/> <br/>Anna</td>', '*Critique CLub* Your idea here is very good, it would make for some good photojournalism but it is to soft.  You need to sharpen it up a bit.  Tools was the challenge subject and the tool in this case appears to be a baseball bat, and it should be the main subject not a blurry little area of the photo. Looking at the photo in a fast glance many voters would have given you a low score not seeing the baseball bat.  Might I strongly suggest you don''t use "Untitled" as a subject.  I feel a photo without a title is missing part of it''s personality as well as part of the photographers personality.  As I look at it I would easily title it something like "Meet my brother the Louisville Slugger" Simply knowing most people know that a Louisville Slugger is a bat, it would make the average voter stop and realize there was a bat in the photo and that is what you wanted the voter to recognize as the tool. Anna', '2003-09-09 21:12:07.000000', NULL, 0),
